+## Durable payers or vanity reach
+
+| Signal | Deals | Source |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Ended or lapsed | Spotify × Headspace (2021–2022); free Calm on Samsung TVs (ended 2021-05-27); Calm absent from AA's 2025 partners | [Headspace help](https://help.headspace.com/hc/en-us/articles/231729627-Can-I-Link-or-Unlink-to-a-Bundled-Subscription-with-Spotify); [Samsung support](https://samsung.com/us/support/answer/ANS10005231); [AA newsroom 2025](https://news.aa.com/news/news-details/2025/American-brings-more-learning-and-laughs-to-the-skies-MKG-OB-11/default.aspx) |
+| Renewed or expanded | Hyatt × Headspace (2019 → 2023 → 2024); Headspace universities (300+ by 2024); Fitbit Premium still lists Calm | [Hospitality Net](https://www.hospitalitynet.org/news/4124044/hyatt-strengthens-holistic-wellbeing-commitment-for-colleagues-guests-and-members); [Behavioral Health Business](https://bhbusiness.com/2024/06/12/headspace-targets-college-students-with-uwill-partnership/); [Google Store](https://store.google.com/gb/product/fitbit_premium?hl=en-US) |
+
+On public evidence, the channels most likely to create durable payers are those where **the user enters their own payment method** (estimate, from deal design and renewals, not outcome data):
+
+1. carrier-billed add-ons (Singtel);
+2. trial codes that auto-renew at retail (Hyatt);
+3. data integrations that need the app's own subscription (Headspace × Oura).
+
+Free-for-all placements (seatbacks, in-room TVs, free TV apps, possibly whole-base telco perks) look more like vanity reach. No company has published the conversion behind either side.
